	(text "These observations identify checks to make on the real wiring; photographs cannot verify continuity.\n\nWIRING AND CONNECTORS\n-  A red and a black wire share one lilac connector (IMG_3422): the colour code is broken.\n-  Two connectors on the 12 V socket's centre terminal (3482); one may sit on the stud (poor contact).\n-  One yellow connector on the USB socket body has no visible wire.\n-  Red wire ends at the button corner, one with a bare tip (IMG_3445, 3502): live if not in the button.\n-  Oversized blue connectors crimped on thin wire; some barrels hold two wires.\n\nFUSE AND CABLE SIZES\n-  Verify the installed fuse value and actual conductor sizes.\n-  Check protection against the ratings of every conductor, connector and load.\n-  Wire sizes estimated from photographs are not sufficient for this check.\n\nSAFETY AND PLACEMENT\n-  Bare 12 V centre stud and brass nut: a short if they touch the seat-belt buckle\n    (car earth) when the panel is turned over on the seat.\n-  Cables and the loose LED head lie across the EV MODE, drive-mode and EPB/HOLD\n    switches and the gear gate (3484); others run near the seat rails and buckle.\n-  Check that the wings do not block the seat-belt buckles or seat adjustment.\n-  If the outlet is live all the time, standby draw (controller, USB ring, Qi pad)\n    slowly drains the car's small 12 V battery.\n\nA proposed protection revision is on a separate sheet. It is not installed or validated."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 147.32 178.435 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.75 1.75)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "30e0abc8-c239-5ffa-bc05-32f6e320fa8f")
	)
